Durarara!! AU
Chapter 35 – Part 1
Anri sat with the blank television and the shut bedroom and the shape of six masks still behind her eyes.
“He met the boy they call Carlos in Mexico.” Her voice stayed even. Thinking out loud, not opening a file. “I wonder if the others have similar stories. And if that’s the reason Curtis and them called themselves Undead.”
Rio looked at the door like the answer might be standing in the hall.
“A name you pick after the worst year,” she said. “Not a band name. A refusal.”
Anri nodded once.
“They keep it till they die. He said that. Like a dove and a grenade.” She did not reach for the USB. “We don’t get to finish the roster. We just… wonder.”
They wondered.
The apartment did not tell them.
Little did either girl know.
Carlos had not joined a crew. He had been left standing in daylight with no father and no one coming. Curtis and Justice were fourteen and already a two-person country. They did not adopt him like a project. They taught him how to feed himself, how to move, how to not walk back into a building. He stayed because staying was the first thing that had a tomorrow attached.
Leona came earlier than Mexico, on the edge of fifteen — a talent-show lens, a girl who filmed first and trusted second. She had been there long enough to document a plane and a garage and a jump. Long enough to already dislike a blonde in an aisle. She was not a rescue. She was the one who kept the record.
Russia was a wrong door.
Curtis and Justice had wanted the country. They did not enter it the way tourists enter things. The details of that crossing did not belong on a living-room table. What belonged was the German boy they found on the other side of a bad idea — Adolf, whose name was a joke the universe had played on a person who hated everything the joke implied. The place they were put was supposed to break people. It did not break them the way Mexico had. The white smoke had already woken. What should have been a grave became, in their telling later, almost a laugh they were not proud of. They came back out with one more brother.
By the time that German door closed, Carlos was no longer only a boy on a shoulder. There was a girl with him.
Maria.
California beaches. A small boat that should not have made it. A North Korean daughter whose parents had gotten her as far as water and then become a question with no answer — alive or not, still on the other side of a border she could not recross. Carlos found her the way the Undead seemed to find everyone: too late for the easy version, just in time for the living one.
Six people.
Not a gang with a color.
A collection of leftovers who refused to stay dead in the places that had tried it.
They named it later, when the masks came out and the cameras started meaning something. Undead. Not because they could not be killed. Because they had already been through the part that should have ended them and they were still taking pictures.
Anri, on the couch, only had a wallpaper and a wonder.
Rio only had a hope that Ikebukuro was changing his mind.
Neither girl had the beach.
Neither girl had the German door.
Neither girl had a fourteen-year-old teaching a younger boy how to be a person after a parking lot.
They sat with the name anyway.
On a furnished floor in Ikebukuro, Anri Sonohara asked
why a crew called itself a refusal.
Rio Kamichika called it the year after the worst year.
The story they did not have was six leftovers learning how to stay alive
in each other’s company. Curtis Nightstar had left that country without a goodbye.
The masks on his screens were still waiting to find Japan.
Somewhere in California. L.A. Afternoon light through cheap blinds. A laptop on a kitchen table that had seen too many takeout nights since a funeral that never got a body.
Leona had the volume up.
A street in a city that was not this one. A borrowed guitar. A crowd that did not know the vow they were clapping for. A masked busker, dime-a-dozen until the hook landed.
Her face had already gone wrong.
“What the hell?” She scrubbed the timeline back. Played the chorus again. “Normally a masked busker. A dime a dozen. But that song doesn’t belong to anyone. That’s a song about the Undead. And the voice is his.”
Adolf came in from the hall, coffee, German vowels still in his mouth even when the English was clean.
“What are you looking at, love?”
Leona turned the screen.
“Is this Curtis?”
Adolf went pale.
Not theatrical. The color just left. The name they had been saying in the past tense sat down in a kitchen in Los Angeles and refused to be past.
Leona’s fingers were already moving.
“Look. There’s more.”
The next clip was daylight and a circle of strangers. A bulky man. A bucket of money. A taller foreigner who put his hands in his pockets like the fight was a joke, then stopped joking when a girl got called a name. The camera work was ugly. The ending was not.
Leona stood so fast the chair scraped.
Furious. Upset. Both at once, no room to pick.
“That son of a bitch is alive. All this time.” Her voice cracked on the second sentence. “When we found Justice I thought— him as well.”
The front door. Keys. Beach-salt still in the air.
Carlos first, Maria half a step behind him.
“Chica.” Carlos’s eyes found the laptop, then Leona’s face. “What’s going on?”
Leona did not soften it.
“Curtis.” She jabbed the screen. Ikebukuro pavement. A red circle of a visor in a thumbnail she had not even opened yet. “He’s in Japan. He’s been in Japan. He’s singing our dead-name on a street like we aren’t here losing our minds.”
Maria’s hand found Carlos’s sleeve.
Adolf had not blinked.
“…We buried a hole,” he said, quiet, precise. “We did not bury him. We assumed. That is not the same.”
Carlos leaned in. The busker clip was still paused on a mouth the mask could not quite hide if you had grown up hearing it yell keep it undead across a parking lot.
“Homes.” It came out wrecked. “That’s him. That’s the dove. That’s—” He cut himself off. Looked at Maria. Looked at Adolf. Looked at Leona like the camera girl had finally filmed the thing that mattered. “He’s alive.”
Leona’s laugh was not a laugh.
“He’s alive and he left. No goodbye. Justice dies and he gets on a plane and he lets us think the forest ate the other one too.” Her eyes were wet and mean with it. “I’m going to kill him. After I hug him. After I make him say it to our faces.”
Maria’s voice was small.
“Japan.”
Adolf set the coffee down untouched.
“Then Japan is no longer a rumor.” He glanced at the fight video, at the way Curtis had ended it, at the crowd cheering a boy they had once packed onto a plane. “He is not hiding very well for a ghost.”
Carlos dragged a hand over his mouth.
“We find the city. We find the street. We don’t go in like a hit.” A beat, softer, the boy from a parking lot still in him. “He told me my dad was gone. He doesn’t get to tell us he is gone and be wrong.”
Leona stared at the paused guitar.
The Undead, still behind, four out of six, a kitchen in L.A. that had been too quiet for thirty days and change.
On a laptop that should have been a busker compilation
and nothing else, Leona found a voice that did not belong to a stranger.
Adolf went pale. Carlos named the dove. Maria held on.
Curtis Nightstar was alive in Ikebukuro — and the crew he had not said goodbye to
had just woken up.
Carlos clapped his hands once, like a plan could be made with noise.
“Then it’s settled, homes. We pack. We go to Japan.” He turned, already bright with the worst kind of confidence. “Maria, mi amor. This is where you will come in.”
Maria stared at him.
Deadpan. Unimpressed. The look of a woman who had crossed more water than he was currently using for geography.
“I’m Korean.”
Carlos blinked.
“Yeah, but—”
“Korean.” She was already walking. “Japan is a different country, Carlos. Different language. Different alphabet. Different everything except the part where you just pointed at Asia like it is one kitchen.”
“I know that—”
“You do not.”
They went down the hall anyway, her voice carrying back in the patient tone of someone who had explained this before and would explain it on the plane if she had to. Carlos followed, arguing with affection, already wrong and already packing in his head.
The kitchen got quiet.
Adolf looked at Leona.
The laptop was still paused on a borrowed guitar.
“Leona.” Careful. No joke in it. “Are you okay?”
Leona did not answer at first. Her eyes were on the screen like if she blinked the clip might turn back into a stranger.
“No.” Honest. Then, smaller: “Yes. I don’t know. He’s alive and I want to break his face and I want to sit on the floor until the past tense comes off him.” She dragged a wrist across her eyes, angry that there was anything there. “We did the funeral math, Adolf. We did it twice. Justice first. Then the quiet. I put Curtis in the same hole because it was easier than believing he would leave us on purpose.”
Adolf’s mouth pulled.
“He is very good at leaving on purpose and calling it protection.” A beat. “That does not make you foolish. It makes him our problem again.”
Leona huffed.
“Maria’s going to teach Carlos the difference between Seoul and Tokyo the whole flight.”
“Yes.” Adolf almost smiled. It did not hold. “And you are going to replay that song until the landing gear comes down. I know you.”
Leona shut the laptop.
Not deleted. Shut.
“I’m okay enough to pack,” she said. “I’m not okay enough to forgive him in a kitchen in L.A.” She glanced at him. “Don’t go pale in Japan. I need you annoying. Not haunted.”
Adolf nodded once. “I will be both. It is my talent.”
From the hall, Maria, still educating: “Hangul is not hiragana, Carlos—” “I know, mi amor—”
Leona stared at the closed computer. Alive. Japan. No goodbye.
The Undead were moving. In a kitchen that had been
too quiet for thirty days, Carlos packed a wrong map
with both hands. Maria began the lecture that would last an ocean.
Adolf asked the only useful question. Leona Stills did not forgive
a ghost for having a pulse.
Ikebukuro did not know it was about to get four more masks.
Leona did not open the laptop again. She talked to the closed lid like it was a person who had already left the room.
“I remember when I first met him and Justice. Two young kids. Thirteen. Stealing from my parents’ shop at night.” A thin, unwilling smile. “When I discovered Curtis I attacked him relentlessly. It didn’t phase him. He already had that abnormal resistance. Justice laughed at me. Curtis apologized. Justice did too, in his own way.”
Adolf listened. He knew better than to fix a memory while it was still arriving.
“Then my own parents passed.” She did not give the year more than that. “It was us three. Shop keys and a camera and two boys who thought a forest had qualified them for the whole country.”
Her thumb worried the laptop’s edge.
“I remember when they went missing. I panicked. I did the math people do when two idiots disappear and the news is ugly. Then they came back different. Mexico. After I found out they were wrongly deported.” The word sat there, bureaucratic and insufficient. “They came back with Carlos. A kid on a shoulder and a story they would not put in a kitchen. And I filmed them anyway because that was the only way I knew how to keep them from vanishing again.”
Adolf’s voice was quiet.
“You kept the record. That is why we have a ghost on a street in Japan.”
Leona’s mouth trembled and went hard.
“I wish we could go to Japan and Justice’s death was a sick joke.” She stared at the table. “But that’s why Curtis left. Because it’s not.”
The kitchen ticked.
Adolf did not tell her she was wrong.
“He ran from the hole,” he said. “Not from us. It feels the same when you are the people in the kitchen.” A beat. “We go anyway. We do not make him explain a funeral in an airport. We make him stand still long enough to be counted.”
Leona wiped her face with the heel of her hand, irritated.
“Thirteen,” she muttered. “Stealing candy and batteries. Now he’s a rumor in another language and we are packing like a joke that grew teeth.”
From the hall, Maria, still mid-lecture:
“—and you do not ask a Korean woman to be your Japanese GPS, Carlos—”
Leona almost laughed. It came out as air.
“Yeah,” she said, to Adolf, to the closed computer, to a boy who had apologized in a shop at thirteen and then survived too much to stay. “I’m okay enough. Let’s go find the son of a bitch.”
Adolf picked up the coffee he had never drunk.
“I will book the tickets. You will not watch the fight video seventeen times.”
“I will watch it eighteen.”
“Of course.”
On a table in Los Angeles, Leona put the first night
back where it belonged — a shop, thirteen, a boy who did not
feel a hit and still said sorry. Justice laughed.
Mexico brought Carlos.
Death was not a joke.
That was why Curtis Nightstar had crossed an ocean without them.
The Undead were done assuming the forest had eaten the other one.